<section class="case-lenses"><p class="eyebrow">CONNECTING THE PERSPECTIVES</p><h2>Experience.<br/>Operations.<br/>Strategy.</h2><div><details class="lens-detail"><summary><span>01</span>Experience<b aria-hidden="true">＋</b></summary><p>Surveys and usability sessions gave context to behavioural signals and helped validate changes.</p></details><details class="lens-detail"><summary><span>02</span>Operations<b aria-hidden="true">＋</b></summary><p>Feature flows, the global Design System and detailed handoff connected design decisions to delivery.</p></details><details class="lens-detail"><summary><span>03</span>Strategy<b aria-hidden="true">＋</b></summary><p>Monthly review, HEART metrics and user-frequency groups informed priorities and adoption discussions.</p></details></div></section>

<section class="outcomes" id="outcomes"><p class="eyebrow">OUTPUTS &amp; OUTCOMES</p><h2>What the work made possible.</h2><p>The programme connected regular measurement with research, tested product improvements and implementation guidance. The original case reports NPS moving from 40 to 78.2 and 50 to 55% of new users remaining active after three months. These are programme-level observations; they do not isolate the effect of an individual intervention or my contribution.</p><div class="reflection"><h3>What I take forward</h3><p>The most useful connection was between the measurement and design work. Keeping both in the same case makes the decision loop visible and avoids treating their shared outcomes as two separate successes.</p></div></section>
